.TH CCOL_CHANNEL_CREATE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_channel_create \- create a two-way communication ccol_channel
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_channel_create(max_size, err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro creates a two-way ccol_channel. Inside, the channel holds two
circular queues. They give communication between an owner thread and the
worker threads. Both directions have the same capacity. The macro uses the
standard malloc and free for memory management.

This macro is the short form of ccol_channel_create_with_mprocs() with NULL
memory management procedures.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
max_size   Maximum number of messages that each direction can hold. One
           circular queue holds each direction. The same
           SIZE_MAX / sizeof(c_message_t) bound therefore applies here.
           ccol_circular_queue_create(3) documents that bound.
err_str    Optional pointer for an error string
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
On success, the macro gives a pointer to the new ccol_channel.
On failure, it gives NULL.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
All operations are thread-safe.
You must destroy the channel with ccol_channel_destroy() after you finish
with it.
The channel picks the direction itself from the ID of the thread that
calls it.
The owner thread is the thread that called ccol_channel_create(). It must
stay alive for all the time that the ccol_channel is in use. To route a
message, the channel compares the ID of the thread that calls it against
the ID that it took at creation time. After the first owner exits, the OS
can give the same ID to a new, unrelated thread. The channel then routes
that thread as the owner too, and it reports nothing.
There are two supported ways to combine fork(2) with a ccol_channel. The
first way is to call fork(2) before you create the ccol_channel. The second
way is to create the ccol_channel, then call fork(2) and immediately call
exec(3). One pattern is not supported: a program continues to run with no
exec(3) in between while a ccol_channel from before the fork is still live.
On top of that rule, the library adds a defense in depth. The child never
inherits a lock in the locked state from another thread that held it at the
instant of the fork. Also, neither queue below the channel owns a thread of
its own. A ccol_channel that a child inherits in that way therefore stays
fully usable.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_channel_create_with_mprocs (3),
.BR ccol_channel_destroy (3)
